Z||000019||V8Fiero||03-25-2000||12:30 PM||||I have purchased and installed the Archie V8 Kit myself. Look at it this way. For about the same price of a replacement 4cyl you can have all the power of a small block chevy.<P>Mine is a stock 350 hooked to the stock fiero automatic transmision. One thing though when installing the kit is DO NOT unhook the rear strut bolts from the spindles. You will have problems with realignment.  Mine was so out of whack it would pull the passenger side axle out of the transmision. I have read alot about the pros and cons of fiero V8 installs. But now that I have one, I wouldnt sell it for anything. WAY TO MUCH FUN&gt;   Later||||reg||10
Z||000020||Oreif||03-25-2000||02:23 PM||||V8Fiero ~ If you by the MOOG strut bolt kit, It has the lower strut bolt with an offset washers. This aids in aligning the rear struts. You just turn it and it adjusts the strut accordingly. The 84 to 87 fieros did not have this ability from the factory. I put on KYB struts with the alignment bolts and alignment is so easy you can to it with a 24" level. (caster/camber adjustment)||||reg||6
Z||000021||mwbackus||03-25-2000||04:32 PM||||Oreif:<BR>Where did you get the strut bolt kit? Do you happen to have a part number?||||reg||5
Z||000022||lowCG||03-25-2000||04:44 PM||||My factory struts are slotted enough to get lots of camber,thought they're all that way.<BR>A level works pretty good for getting the car on the road,but a machine alignment is probably a good idea,I found this out after I had mine checked on a machine,worked better after as well.||||reg||5
Z||000023||Oreif||03-25-2000||05:58 PM||||Part # 57516 From the Fiero Store price is 15.55. This is just the lower bolts that have the offset. MOOG calls them "strut cam bolts" and their part number is K5283. I never checked them out at a local auto parts store due to I bought the entire KYB suspension, ball joints, poly bushings, tie rod ends and sway bars at once from The Fiero Store. (I got a 10% discount too!). From what I understand 88's came with a cam bolt from the factory the earlier years did not. Also you need to make sure the lower hole on the strut is slotted. ||||reg||1
Z||000024||Monkeyman||03-25-2000||06:45 PM||||V8Fiero--How do you figure a V8 conversion is about the same amount of $$ as a replacement Duck?  You can buy a Duck for about $1900 (ready to throw in the car).  The conversion kit alone is more than that.  Then you have to buy the motor.  Do you have some inside info you'd like to share??? <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smile.gif">||||reg||1
Z||000025||88formula||03-25-2000||07:36 PM||||Those big old ancient Detroit pieces of iron with 8 cylinders have to hurt the fiero stability/handling.||||reg||1
Z||000026||Oreif||03-25-2000||09:19 PM||||The handling of a V-8 fiero is not affected by weight. Just put 88 pounds in the trunk. This will actually be worse than a V-8 because the weight will be on the rear side of the axles. Then drive your fiero around and see how much it affects handling. The difference is minimal. Of course the amount of gas you give it will cause the back end to break traction. I had a ride in an 88 formula and we went whipping around a few corners and it still handled the same. How about just add a passenger to the car that will add 150 to 200 (if it's a guy) and see how much the handling is affected. Most people think the weight difference is high but it's only 88 pounds from V-6 to V-8. Add poly bushings and rear sway bar and it will handle even better than a stock V-6! ||||reg||1
Z||000027||batboy||03-27-2000||12:11 AM||||I have three Fieros, one of which is a V8 conversion. I'm speaking from experience, it does affect high performance handling. Lots of torque steer with the stock suspension setup if you jump on it hard. In normal driving conditions you don't notice much difference though.||||reg||1
Z||000028||Dan86gt3.8||03-27-2000||05:30 AM||||Is striaght line performance any better?||||reg||1
Z||000029||batboy||03-27-2000||08:27 AM||||With over twice the displacement and torque of a 2.8, of course straight line acceleration of a V8 Fiero beats the stock engines, duh!||||reg||1
Z||000030||coyote||03-27-2000||11:31 AM||||Transmissions are a touchy subject with v-8 conversions. Most people cannot bielive that a stock tranny will hold up to ther torque and raw power of a v-8 swap. the getrag and others have held up well thus far, I know of no problems associated with V-8 swaps. If you want to move the intake into the center of the craddle it can be done.Trhat was the basis of the zumalt kit. But you will need new axles for that install. i asume that you are talking about a normally aspirated LM1 type motor. LT's and LS's both require new axles any way. If archie posts here i am un-aware of it. It would suprise me to Zumalt and piaza together, he brings alot of liability with him, there are alot of companies looking for him. Lts and LS's have cam driven water pumps that is what keeps them inside. There is still alot of modifications that will be made though.||||reg||1
Z||000031||Dan86gt3.8||03-27-2000||12:49 PM||||Bat boy seing as I did an engine swap (granted it wasn't a V8), I think that was a retorical question, you know for sh!ts n' gigggles.  and coyote, did archie ever complete the ls1 swap, or is that "TOP SECRET"???????????  If so what is the wieght difference, and what kind of computer does he use to controll the engine?  what kind of quarter miles does a 350 with fuel injection run?||||reg||12
Z||000032||HarryG||03-27-2000||01:09 PM||||For all the "Archie kit" owners out there:<BR>How much clearance do you have to change the alternator/AC belt? Archie's install notes talk about less than 1/4" clearance between the frame and the balancer/pulley, which is more than the belt thickness.  How much actual space is there between the balancer and the frame?<BR>  ||||reg||5
Z||000033||no substitue||03-27-2000||10:22 PM||||going to be starting lt1 swap in  about a week. planning on using archies kit. can anyone tell me what is different on the lt1 compared to any other v8 swap<BR>TIA<P>88 cpe soon to be v8<BR>85gt||||reg||1
Z||000034||coyote||03-27-2000||10:29 PM||||The LS1 is fitted into the car, we are waiting for the flywheel, an educated guess would be about 80lbs heavier. A fuel injected 350 (crate motor) putting out around 300 HP would do low 13's high 12's. HarryG, what kind of motor and is it a serpentine or a 'V' belt.||||reg||1
Z||000035||coyote||03-27-2000||10:40 PM||||there is a big difference between the LM and LT swap. Alot more cutting, new axles, all solid mounts, you need to make sure the altenator and compressor are serpentine, and i think you will need a camaro balancer. if you bought the kit from archie he is pretty supportive on the phone.||||reg||1
Z||000036||HarryG||03-28-2000||01:01 PM||||Coyote: My "balancer/pulley/belt clearance" question is about using Archie's kit to put a plain vanilla 350 SBC into an 86 using a 4spd. and Archie's recommended v-belt.<BR>||||reg||1
Z||000037||Haze_Performance||03-29-2000||06:31 AM||||So the V-belt is the way to go huh?  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/biggrin.gif">||||reg||6
Z||000038||batboy||03-29-2000||11:44 AM||||Well, the V-belt is the easiest. Lots of people don't like the idea of the water pump pulley running outside the engine compartment (inside the rear wheel well behind the tire), but it seems to work ok on the Batmobile.<P>   <IMG SRC="http://sites.netscape.net/batboybain/rearsuspension.jpg"> <p>[This message has been edited by batboy (edited 03-29-2000).]||||reg||1
Z||000039||coyote||03-30-2000||04:02 PM||||you should have a 1/4" to 1/2" between the rail and pulley. should be plenty of room to put in a new belt if needed. remember that the eater pump drive assembly is a serpentine and once the lower pulley is welded it has to be assembled as a unit.||||reg||1
Z||000040||chris6878||03-30-2000||04:42 PM||||How would an electric water pump work in this situation? Would it allow u to keep everything inside the engine bay or will it not work in this type of application..?||||reg||5
Z||000041||Spyder||03-30-2000||07:24 PM||||You can run an electric pump, but it is not recommended.  So if your in Florida or somewhere similar, don’t do it.  <BR>I have seen someone mount the Stock Pump toward the dog bone and ran hoses and aligned it to be in line with the stock belts. ||||reg||1
Z||000042||V8Fiero||03-30-2000||10:14 PM||||Monkeyman: Well, Being on the low budget that I was on, I kept my eye on the papers for either a rebuilt engine, or a donor car. I found a Caprice with a fresh rebuilt 350 for $400. I purchase the deluxe kit from Archie ($1450). And then alot of elbow grease and a few more bucks for exhaust, and hoses etc. I still have to replace the radiator but you can see where Im going. Keep in mind this is by no means top of the line stuff here but, WOW. does this car scoot. Now gonna just keep adding things such as performance struts, brakes etc. Hope this helps any of you thinking of doing this conversion||||reg||7
Z||000043||Haze_Performance||04-02-2000||05:13 AM||||Is the V8 conversion very hard? I will do it myself if I could save some $$$||||reg||5
